Report the \textbf{rms} of their difference and the Pearson \textbf{correlation} (``corr'') between them. For two sequences $a_n$ and $b_n$ (here the true and the predicted fluctuations) with averages $\bar a$ and $\bar b$ over the holdout,
\begin{equation}
\corr(a,b)=\frac{\sum_n(a_n-\bar a)(b_n-\bar b)}{\sqrt{\sum_n(a_n-\bar a)^2\,\sum_n(b_n-\bar b)^2}} .
\label{eq:explicit-formula-corr}
\end{equation}
It is a number between $-1$ and $+1$; the proof, via the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality, comes in Chapter~\ref{ch:probability}. The value $+1$ means that the predicted fluctuations are an exact increasing linear function of the true ones, and $0$ means no linear relation at all. So corr measures agreement of \emph{shape}, not of size.
